        <p class="profile-detail-region">Culture · Americas</p>
        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Guaraní</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Homeland</span><span class="info-stat-val">Traditionally the land between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parana-river">Paraná River</a> and the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paraguay">Paraguay</a> River in what is now Paraguay, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/misiones-province">Misiones Province</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argentina">Argentina</a>, southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a> once as far east as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-de-janeiro">Rio de Janeiro</a>, and parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uruguay">Uruguay</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolivia">Bolivia</a>; today mainly Argentina, Bolivia, Brazil and Paraguay, with the Eastern Bolivian Guarani in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gran-chaco">Gran Chaco</a> near the Pilcomayo River</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">First encountered by Europeans in the early 16th century; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuit</a> missions from 1610 until the expulsion of the Jesuits in 1767; the Guarani War of 1754–1756; the Bolivian Chiriguanos resisting until the Battle of Kuruyuki in 1892; a living people today</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Known for</span><span class="info-stat-val">the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guarani-language">Guarani language</a>, an official language of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paraguay">Paraguay</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolivia">Bolivia</a>; communal houses (malocas); the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuit</a> missions, which held more than 100,000 people; the mission army that defeated the Paulista slave raiders in 1641; the Guarani War against the Treaty of Madrid; myths of Ñamandu, Tupã and the Pombero</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">How we know them</span><span class="info-stat-val">Spanish and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuit</a> records, among them Antonio Ruiz de Montoya’s The Spiritual Conquest and the accounts of Martin Dobrizhoffer; letters such as Francisco González de Paniagua’s of 1545 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cacique">caciques</a> of San Luis’s of 1768; mission <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">censuses</a>; myths compiled by the Universidad Nacional de Misiones; modern historians such as Barbara Ganson</span></div>
