    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Before they met Europeans the Guarani called themselves Abá, “men” or “people”; the name Guarani was first applied by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuit</a> missionaries to those who had accepted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>.</li><li>Paulista slave raiders destroyed most of the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuit</a> missions, and within two years 60,000 converts were carried off for sale to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sao-paulo">São Paulo</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-de-janeiro">Rio de Janeiro</a>.</li><li>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guarani-language">Guarani language</a> is an official language of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paraguay">Paraguay</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolivia">Bolivia</a>, and as of 2012 an estimated 90% of the people in Paraguay spoke it.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>When an 800-strong Paulista army attacked the missions in 1641, it was met on the Acaray River by Christian Guarani armed with guns, and its defeat in two battles warded off invasions for ten years.</p></aside>
    </section>

